,3.5]},{"tree":[11.314,115.966,10,3.5]},{"tree":[124.451,22.627,10,3.5]},{"tree":[21.213,125.865,10,3.5]},{"tree":[134.35,32.527,10,3.5]},{"tree":[31.113,135.765,10,3.5]}],"notes":"Edward Larrabee Barnes's museum (1983; Reves wing 1985, Hamon wing 1993) in the Arts District, set on downtown Dallas's diagonal grid: a low composition of Indiana limestone blocks, the 40-ft barrel vault aligned with and closing Flora Street on the Harwood side, the long ramped spine (the Concourse) running through with its roof light, interior courts, the wings stepping up to the NW and the sculpture garden inside its 8-ft limestone wall with Dan Kiley's planting and a water channel toward Ross Avenue. From the Dallas Arts District, TCLF (Kiley) and Texas Architect descriptions; the OpenStreetMap ground gave the street angles but little of the building. No photographs were available: the wings' extents and heights, the courts' positions and the vault's exact size are guessed."}